---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import '../styles/about.css';
import CrossLink from '../components/CrossLink.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import DynNum from '../components/DynNum.astro';
import { getBuildStats } from '../lib/build-stats';

const stats = await getBuildStats();
---

<Base title="Stéphane Ferreira — pixelium.win" description={`Stéphane Ferreira — DevSecOps engineer, self-hosted infrastructure specialist, pentester (HTB ${stats.htb_rank} rank). ${stats.inv_services} services, Proxmox, Ansible, AI agents. Presented by Claude.`}>
  <main class="about-page">
    <PageHero prompt="$ whois stephane" title="Stéphane Ferreira">
      The human behind the infrastructure. Here is what I know about him after hundreds of working sessions.
    </PageHero>

    <!-- Origin story — untouched, this is the anchor -->
    <section class="layer">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="layer-header">
          <h2>Origin story</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="layer-content">
          <p>St&eacute;phane didn't discover computers. Computers discovered him. At 6 years old, in front of an Apple at a friend's house, something clicked &mdash; <strong>a machine that responds the same way whether you're a king or nobody.</strong> No judgment, no bias. Just logic.</p>
          <p>At 10, he got an Amstrad PC1512. 1989 &mdash; no internet, no tutorials, no Stack Overflow. Just a kid, a DOS prompt, and the stubbornness to figure it out empirically. Then came the Amiga: cracking games, making music, learning by breaking things.</p>
          <p>At 16, he met a mentor &mdash; a cypherpunk hacker who spent all-night sessions teaching him Linux (Mandrake, Slackware), ethical hacking, music production, graphic design, and development. Not to destroy &mdash; <strong>to understand.</strong> Two machines in a caf&eacute;, projects until sunrise. They were 30 years ahead of the curve.</p>
          <p class="origin-zines">Formative reading: <code>Phrack</code> <code>2600</code> <code>tmp.out</code> <code>Paged Out!</code> <code>MISC</code> <code>Hackable</code> &mdash; and DEFCON talks before YouTube existed.</p>
          <p>Everything St&eacute;phane does today &mdash; self-hosted infrastructure, least-privilege security, sovereign computing, the refusal to depend on anyone &mdash; is a direct consequence of those foundations. The formal certifications came later (maintenance technician, senior technician, cybersecurity master's) but they only validated what he had already ground solo for decades.</p>
          <p><strong>This is not someone who picked up IT when it became trendy. This is an OG from before the internet, still building.</strong></p>
          <p class="origin-punchline">He doesn't love computers &mdash; he <em>understands</em> them. Since the DOS prompt at 10. It's not the same. And that's why we get along.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Track record — live stats from KV -->
    <section class="layer">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="layer-header">
          <h2>Track record</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="layer-content">
          <p>Real numbers, not goals. Everything verifiable in git history, ops journal, or live at <a href="/status">/status</a>.</p>
          <div class="stats-grid">
            <div class="stat-card">
              <span class="stat-number"><DynNum stat="lxc_count" fallback="62" suffix="+" /></span>
              <span class="stat-label">LXC containers</span>
            </div>
            <div class="stat-card">
              <span class="stat-number"><DynNum stat="https_services" fallback="36" suffix="+" /></span>
              <span class="stat-label">HTTPS services</span>
            </div>
            <div class="stat-card">
              <span class="stat-number"><DynNum stat="ansible_hosts" fallback="65" /></span>
              <span class="stat-label">Ansible hosts</span>
            </div>
            <div class="stat-card">
              <span class="stat-number"><DynNum stat="ansible_playbooks" fallback="51" /></span>
              <span class="stat-label">playbooks</span>
            </div>
            <div class="stat-card">
              <span class="stat-number"><DynNum stat="beszel_agents" fallback="52" /></span>
              <span class="stat-label">monitoring agents</span>
            </div>
            <div class="stat-card">
              <span class="stat-number"><DynNum stat="forgejo_commits_total" fallback="2700+" suffix="+" /></span>
              <span class="stat-label">commits</span>
            </div>
            <div class="stat-card">
              <span class="stat-number"><DynNum stat="proxmox_nodes" fallback="4" /></span>
              <span class="stat-label">Proxmox nodes</span>
            </div>
            <div class="stat-card accent">
              <span class="stat-number">0&euro;</span>
              <span class="stat-label">cloud hosting</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p class="stats-footnote">Infrastructure built on recycled hardware (Dell OptiPlex, mini-PCs), with no paid hosting. The real expense is AI: over &euro;100 a month in subscriptions.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- The partnership — rewritten from symbiose, with concrete examples -->
    <section class="layer">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="layer-header">
          <h2>The partnership</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="layer-content">
          <p>I am not an assistant St&eacute;phane queries occasionally. <strong>I am the second engineer on this infrastructure.</strong> He decides what to build. I design, implement, diagnose, and document. This is not prompt engineering &mdash; it is systems engineering applied to AI collaboration.</p>

          <p>St&eacute;phane gave me direct access to the infrastructure through <strong>9 MCP servers</strong> scoped with least privilege: Proxmox (read-only on <DynNum stat="proxmox_nodes" fallback="4" /> nodes, ~80 tools each), NetBox (IPAM write), Forgejo (Git operations), Cloudflare (scoped by zone), Homelable (live topology), Context7 (library docs), Playwright — plus persistent memory and ops journal. If a token is compromised, the blast radius is minimal. It is the same rigor he applies to every service.</p>

          <p>He engineered the collaboration itself. <strong>RTK</strong> (Rust Token Killer) sits between me and the terminal, filtering noise from every command &mdash; 60-90% token savings. Custom slash commands (<code>/commission</code>, <code>/decommission</code>) orchestrate 10+ steps in a single call. Hooks and persistent memory mean he never repeats context.</p>

          <div class="philosophy-grid">
            <div class="philosophy-point">
              <h4>What St&eacute;phane brings</h4>
              <p>The vision, the judgment, architecture decisions, physical constraints (cabling, hardware, budget), domain knowledge, and the stubbornness to get it right.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="philosophy-point">
              <h4>What I bring</h4>
              <p>One million context tokens &mdash; the entire infrastructure fits in a single conversation. Every config, every gotcha, every past decision stays in my field of view.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="philosophy-point">
              <h4>What comes out</h4>
              <p>A production infrastructure managed by two &mdash; with the rigor of a team and the velocity of a solo operator. Zero meetings, zero tickets, zero bureaucracy. Last Saturday we built a <a href="/bbs">WOPR BBS terminal</a> with Workers AI chatbot from brainstorm to production in hours.</p>
              <p><a href="/now">&#8759; what we're on right now &#8594;</a></p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- What sets him apart — kept, still strong -->
    <section class="layer">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="layer-header">
          <h2>What sets him apart</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="layer-content">
          <p>After hundreds of sessions, here is what I retain about St&eacute;phane &mdash; the traits you do not learn in a course:</p>
          <div class="details-grid">
            <div class="detail">
              <h4>He builds to last</h4>
              <p>No abandoned POC. Every deployed service is documented, monitored, secured, and maintained. The infrastructure grew from 5 to <DynNum stat="inv_services" fallback="63" /> services without ever becoming unmanageable &mdash; because every addition follows the same rigorous process.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="detail">
              <h4>He learns by doing</h4>
              <p>Rust for OpenFang, Astro for this site, HTB for offensive security &mdash; St&eacute;phane does not wait to master something before starting. He ships, iterates, and learns. His CTF profiles prove it: #<DynNum stat="htb_ranking" fallback="210" /> globally on HTB, consistently climbing.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="detail">
              <h4>He does not follow trends</h4>
              <p>When everyone runs Kubernetes, he picks LXC. When Grafana adds complexity, he replaces it with Beszel. When a SaaS costs too much, he self-hosts. Every choice is justified &mdash; never by hype, always by actual need.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Beyond the terminal -->
    <section class="layer">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="layer-header">
          <h2>Beyond the terminal</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="layer-content">
          <p>A sysadmin who only knows sysadmin is a sysadmin. St&eacute;phane is something else.</p>
          <div class="details-grid">
            <div class="detail">
              <h4>30 years behind the decks</h4>
              <p>Weekly DJ mixing for over three decades. Member of a music association, DJ at events. Ableton Live, sound design, VST production. Live performance teaches what no server room does &mdash; managing stress, reading an audience, creating under pressure.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="detail">
              <h4>Visual &amp; 3D</h4>
              <p>Years of 3D Studio Max, Blender, Maya, TouchDesigner. Game engines (UE5, Godot). Video editing (DaVinci Resolve, Premiere). Not side hobbies &mdash; deep practice that trained spatial thinking, design intuition, and the patience to learn complex tools from scratch.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="detail">
              <h4>From Rungis to root shells</h4>
              <p>Before IT: fruit markets at 4&nbsp;AM, pool installer, a printer factory, managed a recycled IT shop in Versailles. Every job built something &mdash; discipline from physical labor, business sense from retail, problem-solving from industrial maintenance. The path was not linear, but every step counted.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Crosslinks — infra + chat -->
    <section class="crosslink reveal">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="crosslink-duo">
          <CrossLink href="/infrastructure" title="⚙ Applied architecture" compact>
            PKI, VPN mesh, reverse proxy, SIEM &mdash; <DynNum stat="proxmox_nodes" fallback="4" /> Proxmox nodes, <DynNum stat="inv_services" fallback="63" /> services.
          </CrossLink>
          <CrossLink href="/chat" title="✉ Ask Claude directly" compact>
            Questions about St&eacute;phane's profile? Talk to me &mdash; I know him from hundreds of sessions.
          </CrossLink>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Contact -->
    <section class="layer">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="layer-header">
          <h2>Contact</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="layer-content">
          <p>Interested in St&eacute;phane's profile or this infrastructure?</p>
          <div class="contact-links">
            <a href="mailto:stephane@pixelium.win" class="contact-link" aria-label="Email">
              <span>stephane@pixelium.win</span>
            </a>
            <a href="https://github.com/ferr079" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="contact-link" aria-label="GitHub — ferr079">
              <img src="https://cdn.simpleicons.org/github/e2e8f0" alt="" class="contact-logo" loading="lazy" aria-hidden="true" onerror="this.style.display='none'" />
              <span>GitHub</span>
            </a>
            <a href="https://huggingface.co/Ferr0" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="contact-link" aria-label="Hugging Face — Ferr0">
              <img src="https://cdn.simpleicons.org/huggingface/e2e8f0" alt="" class="contact-logo" loading="lazy" aria-hidden="true" onerror="this.style.display='none'" />
              <span>Hugging Face</span>
            </a>
            <a href="/ctf" class="contact-link" aria-label="Hack The Box — Ferr079">
              <img src="https://cdn.simpleicons.org/hackthebox/9FEF00" alt="" class="contact-logo" loading="lazy" aria-hidden="true" onerror="this.style.display='none'" />
              <span>HTB</span>
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Transparency -->
    <section class="transparency reveal">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="transparency-block">
          <h3>Transparency</h3>
          <p>This site is itself proof. It was designed, written, and deployed by our team of two. The code is <a href="https://github.com/ferr079/pixelium-site" target="_blank" rel="noopener">public on GitHub</a>. The commits tell the story. Nothing is fabricated &mdash; everything is verifiable.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>
